            <h3>A word on AI...</h3>
            <p>
                I use AI the way I'd use any other tool that saves time. It's good at the
                straightforward work: boilerplate, repetitive refactors, test scaffolding, the
                first pass at something I already know the shape of. Used properly it makes me
                faster and frees up time for the parts that actually need thought.
            </p>
            <p>
                What I don't do is hand over ownership. Anything pushed under my name is my
                responsibility, so I read every line of it, and if I can't explain why it's
                written that way then it doesn't go out. The architecture and the approach are
                mine to work out, and I put real effort into leading how a thing gets built
                rather than handing that decision to a model.
            </p>
            <p>
                I've spent a lot of time reviewing pull requests, a good share of them full of AI
                written code, so I've seen what happens when it goes in unchecked. Merge all of it
                without an engineer properly thinking it through and I can guarantee you end up
                with a mountain of tech debt, a nightmare of security vulnerabilities, and features
                so fragile that anything you plan to build on top of them turns into a problem.
            </p>
